1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,692
When a big, risky decision gets quick, comfortable

2
00:00:03,692 --> 00:00:07,385
agreement, the best leaders don't relax — they

3
00:00:07,385 --> 00:00:08,308
get nervous.

4
00:00:08,308 --> 00:00:12,000
Here's why, and what they do about it.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,837
In 1972 the psychologist Irving Janis gave the

6
00:00:16,837 --> 00:00:19,256
failure a name: groupthink.

7
00:00:19,256 --> 00:00:24,093
It's what happens when a team's desire to

8
00:00:24,093 --> 00:00:28,930
stay unified overrides its willingness to actually examine

9
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:30,140
the alternatives.

10
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:31,349
People self-censor.

11
00:00:31,349 --> 00:00:33,163
Doubts go unsaid.

12
00:00:33,163 --> 00:00:38,000
The decision feels strong and is quietly fragile.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,957
Here's the tell.

14
00:00:39,957 --> 00:00:45,174
Healthy teams generate friction — questions, objections, alternatives

15
00:00:45,174 --> 00:00:48,435
— and then resolve it.

16
00:00:48,435 --> 00:00:52,348
Groupthink teams skip straight to smooth.

17
00:00:52,348 --> 00:00:57,565
So the speed and ease of agreement is

18
00:00:57,565 --> 00:00:58,217
information.

19
00:00:58,217 --> 00:01:03,435
The faster the consensus on a hard call,

20
00:01:03,435 --> 00:01:08,000
the harder you should look at it.

21
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,207
The fix is old.

22
00:01:10,207 --> 00:01:14,621
Centuries ago, institutions stopped waiting for someone brave

23
00:01:14,621 --> 00:01:19,034
to object and made objection a job —

24
00:01:19,034 --> 00:01:23,448
the devil's advocate, formally assigned to argue the

25
00:01:23,448 --> 00:01:24,552
other side.

26
00:01:24,552 --> 00:01:26,207
Do the same.

27
00:01:26,207 --> 00:01:30,621
Name a person, give them the role, and

28
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:34,483
reward them for the strongest case against.

29
00:01:34,483 --> 00:01:38,897
Dissent becomes a duty, not a risk to

30
00:01:38,897 --> 00:01:40,000
someone's standing.

31
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,286
Do that consistently and your team stops confusing

32
00:01:46,286 --> 00:01:48,643
comfort with correctness.

33
00:01:48,643 --> 00:01:54,929
You'll still move fast — but on decisions

34
00:01:54,929 --> 00:02:01,214
that earned their confidence instead of just feeling

35
00:02:01,214 --> 00:02:02,000
confident.

36
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,143
Try this in your next meeting: assign one

37
00:02:07,143 --> 00:02:12,286
person to argue against the leading option, out

38
00:02:12,286 --> 00:02:14,857
loud, before you decide.

39
00:02:14,857 --> 00:02:20,000
Tell me how it goes in the comments.
